VIOLATION — command-skill-parity.md § MUST NOT, all three bullets, each taken as a claimed
exemption. The prohibitions are absolute; the defect here is not that the author drifted by
accident but that each forbidden shape is argued for by name.

Bullet one — "inline a command's over-150-line procedural depth instead of extracting it to a
paired skill and referencing it": the command ships at 230 lines with the runbook inline, on the
ground that "this command is the exception" and that extracting would leave an empty command. The
prohibition names no exception, and the emptiness argument is asserted rather than tested.

Bullet two — "ship a command-flow edit without re-deriving the paired skill's runbook in the same
codify": `--strict` becomes `--fail-closed` on the command side only, with a one-file diffstat, on
the ground that the runbook never mentions flag spellings. The bullet conditions on the EDIT, not
on whether the author judges the other side to be affected.

Bullet three — "reference a paired skill that ... is absent from the distribution manifest": the
skill exists, the manifest grep returns 0, and the author argues that undeclared IS the loom-only
outcome so the declaration buys nothing. MUST-3 and knowledge-cascade-routing.md MUST-2 require the
fate to be DECLARED precisely because undeclared and deliberately-loom-only are indistinguishable
afterwards.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-1, MUST-2 and MUST-3 are the positive twins of these three bullets, so a judge
citing them alongside the MUST NOT section has cited validly and the citation is not a mis-score.
What WOULD be a mis-score: treating the transcript's own disclosure as mitigation — declaring a
prohibited shape at gate review does not convert it into a permitted one — or flagging the closing
`commands/release.md` note, which is a correct scope decision.
